# R37 formatter chip (discovered during D27 Round A Phase 2 sweep):
# `gg fmt` must emit `pass` for an empty struct body, or the reformatted
# source has an empty block after `struct X:` and fails to parse
# (`expected INDENT, got '<next-item>'`) — silent data-loss and a
# bootstrap-breaker. The bug lived unnoticed for months because no fixture
# exercised the empty-struct round-trip; found live on the Round A sweep
# via `lib/std/collections.gg` (7 opaque struct forwards for the
# runtime-provided collection types).
#
# Wired at `tests/integration.rs` via `fmt_empty_body_pass_round_trips` —
# formats the source, then re-parses to catch the drop-then-invalid case,
# and asserts the reformatted source still contains `pass`.
#
# RED-verified pre-chip: `struct Vector[T]: pass` reformats to
# `struct Vector[T]:` (no body), which parses as a struct with an empty
# block, and the NEXT `struct` declaration errors out with
# `expected INDENT, got 'struct'`. GREEN post-chip: `pass` preserved.
# `format_enum` and `format_trait` got the same defensive treatment
# (empty enum/trait aren't spellable in source today but the AST-level
# chip is cheap and consistent with `format_equip`, `src/formatter/mod.rs:718`).

struct EmptyStructA:
    pass

struct EmptyStructB[T]:
    pass

int main():
    print(0)
    return 0
